/// @mainpage clad
///
/// @section main_intro Introduction
/// This documentation describes the @b internal software that makes up clad,
/// not the @b external use of clad. There are no instructions here on how to
/// use clad, only the APIs that make up the software. For usage instructions,
/// please see the user guide.
///
/// @section main_shape How clad is put together
/// clad is a clang plugin. It runs as an AST consumer, finds the calls that
/// ask for a derivative, and generates a function for each of them before the
/// rest of the compiler sees the translation unit. Three layers do that work,
/// and they are the three places to start reading:
///
/// - @ref pipeline decides @em what to differentiate. Clang hands each group
///   of declarations to clad::plugin::CladPlugin, which defers them; once the
///   unit is parsed, clad::DiffScheduler walks it with clad::DiffCollector,
///   which records a clad::DiffRequest for every @c clad:: call it finds.
///   The requests live in a clad::DynamicGraph, which is drained one node at
///   a time -- and grows while it drains, because deriving one function
///   discovers the calls it makes.
///
/// - @ref visitors decides @em how. clad::DerivativeBuilder picks a visitor
///   for the request's mode and the visitor walks the original body, building
///   the derivative's AST as it goes. clad::VisitorBase holds what they share;
///   clad::BaseForwardModeVisitor and clad::ReverseModeVisitor are the two
///   that matter, and the rest are built on them.
///
/// - @ref analyses decides @em what can be left out. A derivative is correct
///   without them and much more expensive: the to-be-recorded analysis is what
///   keeps a reverse sweep from storing every intermediate value.
///
/// Two more groups are support rather than architecture. @ref astutils holds
/// the helpers for building and copying clang AST nodes, and @ref runtime
/// holds the types the generated code and the user's program both link
/// against -- clad::array, clad::tape, clad::CladFunction.
///
/// @section main_caveat Caveat
/// This documentation is generated directly from the source code with doxygen.
/// Since clad is constantly under active development, what you're about to
/// read is out of date!
///
/// @defgroup pipeline The differentiation pipeline
/// @brief Finding the requests and driving them to a derivative.
///
/// Entry is clad::plugin::CladPlugin, an AST consumer. It defers each
/// declaration group, then plans and derives once the unit is parsed. The
/// request graph is what makes the two passes separate: deriving a function
/// adds requests to the graph that is being drained.
///
/// @defgroup visitors Mode visitors
/// @brief Turning one request into the AST of a derivative.
///
/// One visitor per differentiation mode, all sharing clad::VisitorBase.
/// clad::DerivativeBuilder chooses between them from the request's mode.
///
/// @defgroup analyses Static analyses
/// @brief Deciding what the generated code does not have to do.
///
/// Each analysis answers one question about the original function -- which
/// values a reverse sweep will read back, which variables the result depends
/// on -- so the visitors can leave work out.
///
/// @defgroup astutils AST utilities
/// @brief Building, cloning and comparing clang AST nodes.
///
/// @defgroup runtime Runtime support
/// @brief The types the generated code and the caller share.
